## Content lifecycle

Set `content_base64 = filebase64("${path.module}/file.bin")` for a local file, or
`base64encode("text")` for text. This is ordinary standard base64, not JSON.
Uploads use raw file bytes and are subject to `files_max_upload_bytes`.
Before replacing an existing file, the provider reads that setting and compares
the decoded byte count. Oversized content, invalid limits, or an unreadable limit
abort planning before deletion. This replacement preflight additionally requires
`app.settings.read`; ordinary metadata updates and new uploads do not require
that permission.
File content is sensitive in output but remains in Terraform state. Secure the
state backend and keep large files outside this resource when state size matters.

Changes to content, MIME type or location replace the file. Name and parent path
change in place. Do not enable `create_before_destroy` when replacing at the same
path: catalog names are unique. The size preflight cannot reserve storage or
make replacement atomic: a changed limit or storage failure after planning can
still reject the later upload. For content that must remain available during
replacement, upload under a different path and switch consumers before removing
the old file. Out-of-band content changes are detected from the
server checksum and trigger replacement when content is configured.

Creation and replacement validate supplied base64 before modifying the catalog.
A replacement requires known bytes at plan time; resolve generated replacement
content in a separate apply first. New file creation accepts generated content
and validates it when resolved. Removing a configured `content_base64` is
rejected before deletion. To relinquish byte ownership while retaining the file,
remove it from Terraform state, then import it again with content omitted.

Import uses `location_id:entry_id`. Omit `content_base64` to manage only metadata
of an imported file; reads never download its bytes. Supplying content after
import plans replacement. New files always require content, including
`content_base64 = ""` for an empty file. Deletion permanently removes the file.

## Entry metadata

| Field | Type and meaning |
|---|---|
| `id`, `location_id` | Integer entry identity and containing location |
| `name`, `path` | Name and full relative catalog path |
| `kind` | `file` or `folder` |
| `size` | File size in bytes; null for folders |
| `sha256` | Hexadecimal SHA-256 checksum; null for folders |
| `content_type` | Recorded MIME type, or null |
| `uploaded_by` | Uploader identity, or null |
| `created_at`, `updated_at` | Server timestamps |

Requires `app.files.read` on the location. Resource management also requires
`app.files.write` and `app.files.delete` on that location. A visible ancestor
location is navigation only: it does not grant file access. When an entry is not
visible, managed resources retain state and report an error because the API
cannot distinguish deletion from lost scope. Restore permission, or confirm the
entry was deleted and use `terraform state rm` before recreating it.

